1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,156
Tanstack Router is a must-have library when creating multi-page React applications.

2
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:10,372
And it solves one single problem very elegantly, and that is routing.

3
00:00:10,798 --> 00:00:15,364
So what is routing and how can you implement it using Tanstack Router?

4
00:00:15,534 --> 00:00:18,084
Let's say a user visits your website.

5
00:00:18,414 --> 00:00:21,636
They click on the about link and land in your about page.

6
00:00:21,966 --> 00:00:25,680
Notice how the URL path also changed to slash about.

7
00:00:26,158 --> 00:00:29,840
Similarly, when they click on contact, they land in your contact page.

8
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:33,732
And of course the URL is also updated to slash contact.

9
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:35,332
So what is routing?

10
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,960
Well, it's pretty simple.

11
00:00:37,198 --> 00:00:41,220
It's just matching a URL to the correct UI components.

12
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:46,640
And TANStac Router does this very beautifully using file-based routing.

13
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:57,680
To understand file-based routing, we're gonna go one step back and take a look at how routing was implemented in React before Tansac Router, and that was programmatically

14
00:00:57,840 --> 00:00:58,980
meaning in the code.

15
00:00:59,342 --> 00:01:08,420
So inside the main app component, you would simply import the route component from, for example, React Router DOM, which is another older

16
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,420
routing library, and use it to define your routes.

17
00:01:12,686 --> 00:01:22,240
For example, you can define the first route which matches slash, which is the home page, and then you can pass in your UI component. In this case, this is the home component.

18
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:28,480
You can also define a second path slash about, and here we're gonna pass in the about component.

19
00:01:28,782 --> 00:01:34,240
Similarly, we can define a third page slash contact, and here we're gonna pass in the contact page.

20
00:01:34,414 --> 00:01:39,280
And we can also define a fallback route by providing a not found component.

21
00:01:39,566 --> 00:01:45,120
Luckily, Tansac router takes a different approach, which means you don't have to write all this kind of code.

22
00:01:45,326 --> 00:01:47,940
You can simply take care of your file system.

23
00:01:48,238 --> 00:01:55,920
So inside your source directory, you can simply create a new folder called routes, and here you can define the routes that you want to have.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,160
For example, index.tsx, this is gonna handle the home page, you can have an about page, and this is gonna match slash about.

25
00:02:04,910 --> 00:02:09,764
You can have similarly contact.tsx and this is gonna handle slash contact.

26
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:13,828
By now you're probably already wondering what is inside these files.

27
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:15,280
Well, let's take a look.

28
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:25,360
So this is the content of the homepage, and we start by importing create file route from TANSAC ReactRouter, and we use this to create our route.

29
00:02:25,678 --> 00:02:28,740
And here we're also passing in the path of the route.

30
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:33,828
And of course, we also need to provide the component that we want to show the user.

31
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:44,516
I know at first glance this looks like a lot of tedious code, but actually that's not the case because actually all of this is all generated for you automatically.

32
00:02:44,846 --> 00:02:50,628
So every time that you create a file inside your routes directory, this is the code that is generated for you.

33
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:57,200
And normally you only need to modify this part of the code, just change the component that you want to show to the user.

34
00:02:57,454 --> 00:03:03,172
The cool thing about using files to define the structure of your website is that it comes with a lot of goodies.

35
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:11,748
One of them is the ease of creating nested routes, which means that inside our routes folder you can add another folder,

36
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:19,972
and in this case, this is gonna be a blog, and then we're gonna have an index page inside this blog, and this is gonna match slash blog.

37
00:03:20,174 --> 00:03:23,680
We can also define other routes inside this folder.

38
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:31,652
So another, for example, blog article is gonna be React.js, and this is gonna have the path slash blog slash React.js.

39
00:03:32,014 --> 00:03:41,760
Now obviously, if you have a blog, you don't want to create a new file for every blog article that you have, and for that we can actually take care of path parameters.

40
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:47,076
So here we have a path parameter called slug and we define it by using this dollar sign.

41
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:56,100
And not only is this gonna match slash blog slash react.js, it's also gonna match slash blog slash basically any string that you want to have.

42
00:03:56,622 --> 00:04:07,076
Inside the blog page, you can also get access to this path parameter by using route.use params, and this is gonna have the same name as the one that you defined in your file name.

43
00:04:07,598 --> 00:04:11,936
Now that you have access to the slug, you can use it to query your data.

44
00:04:12,206 --> 00:04:17,444
For example, here we're using React query and we are getting the data and then showing it to the user.

45
00:04:17,616 --> 00:04:25,444
Now, one important thing I want to let you know is that if you are fetching some kind of data, it's best not to do it inside your component.

46
00:04:25,616 --> 00:04:28,176
And there are actually two reasons for that.

47
00:04:28,398 --> 00:04:36,016
So the best way to load any kind of data would be to use this loader parameter that you can pass to create file route.

48
00:04:36,206 --> 00:04:41,216
And here we are just grabbing parameters and getting the blog data.

49
00:04:41,486 --> 00:04:47,936
And inside the blog page or the component, you can access this data using route that use loader data.

50
00:04:48,096 --> 00:04:50,180
And there are actually two reasons for that.

51
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:57,188
First one is that you get access to built-in loading components from 10-Stack Router.

52
00:04:57,454 --> 00:05:06,308
So you can provide a loading UI to be shown to the user while the data is loaded, and you do this by using the loading component property,

53
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:10,736
and you can also provide an error component by using error component.

54
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:15,856
And the cool thing about error and loading components is that you can also define them globally.

55
00:05:16,046 --> 00:05:27,376
So inside your router.tsx file, you can also specify a default pending component and a default error component, which means that you don't have to define these in every page,

56
00:05:27,630 --> 00:05:32,772
you just have to define them once globally, and they're going to be used in every page that is using a loader.

57
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:40,164
Of course, in every page, you also have the possibility to overwrite it by providing a specific error or loading component.

58
00:05:40,430 --> 00:05:46,976
And since we're in this file, you can also turn on scroll restoration, which means that when the user clicks on the back button,

59
00:05:47,056 --> 00:05:52,356
they're going to land in the same place vertically in the website, which is just a very good user experience.

60
00:05:52,910 --> 00:05:57,136
Now, the second reason why you should loaders is that it enables pre-fetching.

61
00:05:57,262 --> 00:06:02,816
Let's go back to our website example when a user wants to navigate to the about page.

62
00:06:02,976 --> 00:06:06,656
They're gonna go and hover over the about link.

63
00:06:06,816 --> 00:06:12,976
And when this happens, this is gonna trigger a pre-fetch of the slash /about route in the background.

64
00:06:13,166 --> 00:06:17,536
And when the user clicks on about, they're gonna land inside the about page directly.

65
00:06:17,742 --> 00:06:19,652
So the data is already loaded.

66
00:06:19,886 --> 00:06:28,096
If you load the data directly in the component, this is going to lead to a visible loading state that then results in the actual UI.

67
00:06:28,256 --> 00:06:33,764
Now let's talk about a different kind of parameters that you can use, and that is search parameters.

68
00:06:34,222 --> 00:06:42,816
Let's say we have a blog with multiple pages, so the user can actually click on the second page and see the second page of your blog entries.

69
00:06:43,182 --> 00:06:51,856
It's a very good practice to store this data in the URL, and for this you can use search parameters, which is just this part of the URL that comes after the question mark.

70
00:06:52,096 --> 00:06:53,860
in this case, we have page equals two.

71
00:06:54,862 --> 00:07:03,296
Using search parameters or moving state to the URL is literally one of the best ways to improve the quality of your website

72
00:07:03,534 --> 00:07:06,176
and just unlocks a lot of features by default.

73
00:07:06,414 --> 00:07:10,436
So let me show you how to define search parameters using Tanstack Router.

74
00:07:10,862 --> 00:07:22,404
Let's go back to our route, and here we're going to import Z from Zod. So Zod is just a runtime validation library, and we can use Zod to define the schema of our search parameters.

75
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:28,416
And here we're just going to say we want to have the field page, and this is going to be a number.

76
00:07:28,590 --> 00:07:34,176
And in case the user didn't provide any page, we're going to have the default value of one.

77
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:43,076
So once we pass our search schema to validate search, we have access to this hook route that use search, and again,

78
00:07:43,406 --> 00:07:46,788
this is type safe, so you cannot access a field that doesn't exist.

79
00:07:47,118 --> 00:07:57,604
So all the parsing is going to be done for you by Tanstack Router and Zod, and you just get the data directly in your component that you can use to load and show the data that you want.

80
00:07:57,856 --> 00:08:06,496
So one of the core components that you have to use when working with Tansta Crowder is the link component, simply because it's too powerful.

81
00:08:06,734 --> 00:08:11,776
So this is how you would normally define a link in your website using the anchor element.

82
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:16,240
And you would simply provide the link inside the href prop.

83
00:08:16,622 --> 00:08:22,240
But in TANSAC router, you should actually use the link component that comes from Tenstack Router.

84
00:08:22,446 --> 00:08:27,360
And it's very similar to the anchor element with just a few key differences.

85
00:08:27,726 --> 00:08:31,200
The first one is that you provide the target using a to prop.

86
00:08:31,598 --> 00:08:40,240
And again, this is type safe, so you cannot just enter any URL here. If you type in something that is invalid, you're just gonna get an error directly in your code editor.

87
00:08:40,334 --> 00:08:48,068
Let's say, for example, we want to navigate to a blog entry, then we can change this to slash blog slash slug.

88
00:08:48,270 --> 00:08:54,720
And here we're directly gonna get an error because we are actually missing a parameter, which is the actual slug.

89
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:03,280
So you have to provide in this parameter basically by using params and passing in an object with all the missing parameters.

90
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:09,920
And the cool thing is that you can also pass in search parameters very similarly by using the search prop.

91
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:16,548
One cool feature that you have to know when using the link component is that it also provides an active props.

92
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:21,920
And these are just props that are gonna be passed to this link when the link matches the current page.

93
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:27,268
So if you have a link to slash blog and you are in slash blog, then all of these props are gonna be applied.

94
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,120
For example, a class name.

95
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:37,924
But personally, the way that I like to use link, especially when you're using, for example, Shut C N, is to just wrap it in another component.

96
00:09:38,222 --> 00:09:43,200
And in this case, I just wrapped it in a button and I pass in as child to the button.

97
00:09:43,470 --> 00:09:50,532
So what you end up with is a component that has the appearance of a button but behaves just like a normal link.

98
00:09:51,054 --> 00:09:58,404
One of the coolest features about Tastack Router, and I cannot make a video about Tastack Router without talking about it, is layouts.

99
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:08,080
Let's go back to our website example, and let's say that in our blog we want to have the same layout in the index page as well as in the blog entry

100
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:08,640
page.

101
00:10:09,006 --> 00:10:17,444
So what we can do here is take advantage of layouts, and we do this by creating a file with the same name, and this is going to be blog.tsx.

102
00:10:18,158 --> 00:10:26,564
This layout file looks exactly the same as a normal file route with one simple difference, and that is that we get to define an outlet.

103
00:10:27,214 --> 00:10:34,340
So an outlet is just a specific component that comes from Tanstack Router, and this is where all the children are going to be rendered.

104
00:10:34,670 --> 00:10:44,676
Now I know this can get a bit confusing, so I have prepared a small exercise that I wanted to go through with you together just to figure out how layouts and components are actually matched with

105
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:45,920
tastack router.

106
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:50,052
So let's say we have a URL slash blog slash ABC.

107
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:58,596
We're gonna first check if there's any match, and we can see that there is a blog.tsx, So we're gonna render a blog layout.

108
00:10:59,022 --> 00:11:07,428
Then we're gonna check there's also a folder with the same name, and inside this folder there is this file, dollar sign $slug, which also matches the URL.

109
00:11:07,662 --> 00:11:10,160
So this is what's going to be rendered to the user.

110
00:11:10,414 --> 00:11:14,880
Now, if you only had /blog, it's going to look actually very similar.

111
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:23,280
First, the blog layout is matched, and then inside the blog directory, the index.tsx file is matched, and we end up with

112
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:27,200
blog layout, and inside of it is going to be the blog index file.

113
00:11:27,438 --> 00:11:33,920
So, with all this knowledge about Tenstack Router, you should be able to create multi page React applications very easily.

114
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:36,644
Thanks again for watching and see you in the next video.

